## Fixed-height example (concept)

```javascript
function VirtualList({ items, itemHeight, height }) {
  const [scrollTop, setScrollTop] = useState(0);
  const visibleCount = Math.ceil(height / itemHeight);
  const start = Math.floor(scrollTop / itemHeight);
  const end = start + visibleCount + 1; // overscan
  const slice = items.slice(start, end);

  return (
    <div
      style={{ height, overflow: "auto" }}
      onScroll={(e) => setScrollTop(e.target.scrollTop)}
    >
      <div style={{ height: items.length * itemHeight, position: "relative" }}>
        <div style={{ transform: `translateY(${start * itemHeight}px)` }}>
          {slice.map((item, i) => (
            <div key={start + i} style={{ height: itemHeight }}>
              {item}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Libraries

| Library | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| `react-window` | Lightweight, fixed/variable size lists and grids |
| `react-virtualized` | Older, more features, heavier |
| `@tanstack/react-virtual` | Headless, flexible |

## Tradeoffs

| Fixed height | Variable height |
| --- | --- |
| Simple math | Measure rows or estimate + correct |
| Fast scroll | More complex, possible jank |

## How to answer in the interview

> "I'd virtualize — compute visible range from scrollTop and row height, render only that slice inside a tall spacer container, add overscan for smooth scroll. For production I'd use react-window or TanStack Virtual. For variable heights I'd measure or use estimated sizes."

## Common mistakes

- No overscan → blank flashes while scrolling fast
- Wrong total scroll height → scrollbar jumps
- Index as key when window shifts without stable item ids

## Key takeaways

- Only mount visible items + small buffer.
- Spacer div sets scroll height; inner list translates with `transform`.
- Variable height needs measurement strategy.

## Interview follow-ups

- **Infinite scroll + virtualization?** — Fetch next page when near end index.
- **Accessibility?** — Ensure keyboard nav and screen reader range announcements.

Before JSX and ES6 classes, React components were written in **ES5** using `React.createClass` or manual constructor functions with `prototype`. Building a **mini React-like class system** tests your understanding of `this`, prototypes, state, and lifecycle — without framework magic.

## Minimal Component base (ES5)

```javascript
function Component(props) {
  this.props = props || {};
  this.state = {};
}

Component.prototype.setState = function (partial, callback) {
  for (var key in partial) {
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(partial, key)) {
      this.state[key] = partial[key];
    }
  }
  this.forceUpdate(callback);
};

Component.prototype.forceUpdate = function (callback) {
  if (typeof this._onUpdate === "function") {
    this._onUpdate(this);
  }
  if (typeof callback === "function") callback();
};
```

