## Render → commit → effects pipeline

```
Render → DOM commit → useLayoutEffect → browser paint → useEffect
```

## useLayoutEffect — prevent visual flicker

```javascript
function Tooltip({ anchorRef }) {
  const tooltipRef = useRef(null);
  const [pos, setPos] = useState({ x: 0, y: 0 });

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    const anchor = anchorRef.current.getBoundingClientRect();
    const tooltip = tooltipRef.current.getBoundingClientRect();
    setPos({
      x: anchor.left + anchor.width / 2 - tooltip.width / 2,
      y: anchor.bottom + 8,
    });
  }, [anchorRef]);

  return <div ref={tooltipRef} style={{ position: 'fixed', left: pos.x, top: pos.y }} />;
}
```

If this measurement ran in `useEffect`, the user would see the tooltip jump from `(0,0)` to the correct position.

## useEffect — default for side effects

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `Count: ${count}`;
}, [count]);

useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  fetch('/api/data', { signal: controller.signal });
  return () => controller.abort();
}, []);
```

## Rule of thumb

> Start with `useEffect`. Reach for `useLayoutEffect` only when you measure or mutate layout and users see a flash.

## Key takeaways

- `useLayoutEffect` = synchronous, blocks paint, layout-critical.
- `useEffect` = non-blocking, preferred for most side effects.
- Both cleanups run before the next invocation and on unmount.

**React 18** introduced a concurrent renderer that can **pause, resume, and prioritize** work instead of blocking the main thread until a full tree is finished.

## React 18 highlights

| Feature | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `createRoot` | New root API (replaces `ReactDOM.render`) enabling concurrent features |
| Automatic batching | Multiple `setState` calls in timeouts, promises, and native handlers batch into one render |
| `useTransition` | Mark state updates as non-urgent (interruptible) |
| `useDeferredValue` | Defer a value until urgent work finishes |
| `Suspense` + `lazy` | Code-split components; show fallback while loading |
| Streaming SSR | Send HTML in chunks (with frameworks like Next.js) |

```javascript
import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';

const root = createRoot(document.getElementById('root'));
root.render(<App />);
```

## Concurrent rendering mental model

React can keep **two versions** of the UI in memory — the current committed tree and a work-in-progress tree. Urgent updates (typing in an input) can interrupt a slow re-render (filtering a huge list).

