## Why order matters

```javascript
// WRONG — conditional hook breaks call order between renders
function Bad({ show }) {
  if (show) {
    const [name, setName] = useState(''); // hook index shifts!
  }
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
}

// CORRECT — hooks always run in the same order
function Good({ show }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  if (!show) return null;
  return <div>{name} {count}</div>;
}
```

## Valid vs invalid

| Valid | Invalid |
| --- | --- |
| Top-level in component body | `if (x) useState()` |
| Inside custom hook (`useAuth`) | Inside `forEach` / `map` |
| | Inside event handler callback |
| | Inside class component |

## ESLint enforcement

`eslint-plugin-react-hooks` (`rules-of-hooks`, `exhaustive-deps`) catches most violations at build time.

## Key takeaways

- Hooks are linked-list slots — position must be identical every render.
- Extract conditional logic **after** hooks, or into separate components.
- Custom hooks can call other hooks; plain helpers cannot.

A component **re-renders** when React needs to reconcile a new version of its output. Props changing alone does not schedule a render — something upstream must trigger it.

## Triggers

| Trigger | Example |
| --- | --- |
| **Own state changes** | `setCount(1)`, `dispatch({ type: 'ADD' })` |
| **Parent re-renders** | Parent state changes → all children render (unless memoized) |
| **Context value changes** | Any consumer of a changed Provider re-renders |
| **`forceUpdate`** | Class components only |
| **Same state value** | React may bail out via `Object.is`, but still may render once |

