## The reference trap

```javascript
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const data = { name: 'Alice' }; // new object every render!

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount((c) => c + 1)}>{count}</button>
      <Child data={data} /> {/* memo is useless — new reference each time */}
    </>
  );
}

// Fix: stabilize with useMemo
const data = useMemo(() => ({ name: 'Alice' }), []);
```

## Custom comparison function

Second argument: `(prevProps, nextProps) => boolean` — return **`true` to SKIP** re-render (opposite of `shouldComponentUpdate`).

```javascript
const OptimizedList = React.memo(
  function OptimizedList({ items }) {
    return <ul>{items.map((i) => <li key={i.id}>{i.name}</li>)}</ul>;
  },
  (prev, next) => prev.items.length === next.items.length
);
```

Use custom comparators sparingly — shallow compare is correct for most cases.

## When memo helps

- Component is **measurably expensive** to render
- Props are **stable** (primitives, memoized objects/callbacks)
- Parent re-renders frequently with unchanged child props

## Key takeaways

- Default: shallow prop comparison.
- Custom comparator returns `true` = skip render.
- Pair with `useMemo` / `useCallback` to stabilize object and function props.

**Profile first, optimize second.** React DevTools Profiler shows which components render and how long they take.

## Step-by-step playbook

| Step | Action |
| --- | --- |
| 1. Measure | React DevTools Profiler, browser Performance tab |
| 2. Reduce unnecessary renders | `React.memo`, `useMemo`, `useCallback` |
| 3. Virtualize long lists | `react-window`, `@tanstack/react-virtual` (see Learnings #4) |
| 4. Code-split routes | `React.lazy` + `Suspense` |
| 5. Defer non-urgent work | `useTransition`, `useDeferredValue` |
| 6. Fix context | Split contexts, memoize Provider value |
| 7. Move work off main thread | Web Workers for heavy computation (rare) |

