## Module Federation sketch

```javascript
// Host webpack.config.js
new ModuleFederationPlugin({
  name: 'host',
  remotes: {
    checkout: 'checkout@http://localhost:3001/remoteEntry.js',
  },
  shared: { react: { singleton: true }, 'react-dom': { singleton: true } },
});

// Host component
const RemoteCart = React.lazy(() => import('checkout/Cart'));
```

## Common challenges

- **Shared state** — event bus, URL as source of truth, or lightweight global store
- **Routing** — host owns top-level routes; remotes own sub-routes
- **Consistent UX** — shared design tokens / component library
- **Dependency deduplication** — enforce single React instance via `shared` config

## Key takeaways

- Trade deployment independence for integration complexity.
- Module Federation is the most common React approach for runtime composition.
- Shared design system and routing contract are as important as the tech choice.

Not all logic belongs in a custom hook. **Hooks call hooks**; plain helpers do pure computation. Mixing them incorrectly causes bugs or unnecessary coupling.

## The useTasks pattern

```javascript
// Pure helper — no hooks, easy to unit test
function generateRandomTasks(count) {
  const verbs = ['Write', 'Review', 'Fix', 'Deploy'];
  return Array.from({ length: count }, (_, i) => ({
    id: crypto.randomUUID(),
    title: `${verbs[i % verbs.length]} task ${i + 1}`,
    done: false,
  }));
}

// Custom hook — owns React state and effects
function useTasks(initialCount = 5) {
  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState(() => generateRandomTasks(initialCount));

  const addTask = useCallback((title) => {
    setTasks((prev) => [...prev, { id: crypto.randomUUID(), title, done: false }]);
  }, []);

  const toggleTask = useCallback((id) => {
    setTasks((prev) =>
      prev.map((t) => (t.id === id ? { ...t, done: !t.done } : t))
    );
  }, []);

  const regenerate = useCallback(() => {
    setTasks(generateRandomTasks(initialCount));
  }, [initialCount]);

  return { tasks, addTask, toggleTask, regenerate };
}
```

