**Question:** what is `React.memo()` for, how does it differ from `useMemo()`, and when would you pick one over the other?

Mechanics for each are in [Understanding React.memo](/react-learnings/22) and [useMemo: Why It Might Not Behave As Expected](/react-learnings/21). This entry is the decision.

## The one-line distinction

**`React.memo` memoizes a component's output. `useMemo` memoizes a value.**

Different units, different triggers, different places in the code:

| | `React.memo(Component)` | `useMemo(fn, deps)` |
|---|---|---|
| Kind | Higher-order component | Hook |
| Wraps | A component definition, at module scope | An expression, inside a component |
| Caches | Rendered output (the whole subtree) | A single computed value |
| Re-evaluates when | Props change (shallow compare) | A dependency changes (`Object.is`) |
| Prevents | A child re-rendering | A recomputation |

