## Choosing

**Use `React.memo` when** a child component is expensive to render and its props usually don't change while its parent re-renders often. A chart, a large table, a heavy list row.

**Use `useMemo` when** a computation inside a component is expensive and its inputs rarely change — or when a value must keep a stable reference.

**Use both when** you're memoizing a component that receives object, array, or function props. This is the common case, and the reason the two get confused:

```javascript
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  return items.map((i) => <Row key={i.id} item={i} onSelect={onSelect} />);
});

function Parent({ rawItems, filter }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Without these two, memo compares a new array and a new function
  // every render and never skips anything.
  const items = useMemo(() => rawItems.filter(filter), [rawItems, filter]);
  const onSelect = useCallback((id) => console.log(id), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList items={items} onSelect={onSelect} />
    </>
  );
}
```

`React.memo` alone would do nothing here. `useMemo` alone would do nothing here. Together they stop the list re-rendering on every click.

**`useCallback` is the same tool for functions** — `useCallback(fn, deps)` is exactly `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`.

## The answer that scores in an interview

> They operate on different units. `React.memo` wraps a component and skips its re-render when props are shallow-equal; `useMemo` caches a value inside a component and recomputes it when dependencies change. In practice they're used together — `memo` on the child, `useMemo`/`useCallback` on the object and function props feeding it, since a shallow compare fails on any inline object. Either one alone usually accomplishes nothing.

Then the tradeoff line:

> Neither is free — both cost a comparison on every render plus a retained cache. I'd reach for restructuring first, since moving state down or passing children as props removes the re-render entirely rather than paying to detect it.

