Most React architecture pain comes from state living at the wrong level. The fix isn't a better library — it's a **ladder**, where you start at the bottom and climb only when the current rung genuinely stops working.

To put it simply: put state as close as possible to where it's used, and move it up only when something forces you to.

## The Problem

Two failure modes, and teams tend to swing between them.

**Everything global.** A store holding form inputs, modal open/closed flags, and cached API responses. Every component is coupled to a single object, nothing can be reasoned about in isolation, and half the store is stale server data nobody is invalidating.

**Nothing shared.** The same state duplicated in four components, prop-drilled through six layers, drifting out of sync.

The ladder gives you a defensible reason to be at whichever rung you're on — which is exactly what an interviewer is probing for when they ask "how would you manage state here?"

